  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA cluster of thunderstorms that has developed on the nose of a \nmodest low level jet will move through the middle of the forecast\narea through the early morning hours. This activity has been pretty\ncellular and progressive, so the flood threat looks to be low but non\nzero. Thus will let the flood watch continue until expiration. At\nthis point, do not expect to extend it, even though additional\nconvection is likely during the day. \n\nIt appears that another impulse will cause more thunderstorms develop\nand move across the area this afternoon. This will also be \nassociated with a pre-frontal trough. Precipitable water will still \nbe high, but the warm cloud layer will not be quite as deep. So there\nmay still be locally heavy rain, but the flooding threat appears to \nbe more isolated. In addition, with moderate instability there could \nbe some more robust storms that could produce strong to damaging \nwinds.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nLow amplitude short wave will push a cold front into the area\ntonight. This will become elongated in a north northeast to south\nsouthwest orientation which will cause the boundary to be slow to\nmove all of the way through the region. There will still be some\npotential for showers and storms until this front clears. \n\nBroad long wave trough will move into the Great Lakes towards mid\nweek. This will push another cold front through late Wednesday into\nWednesday night. There will be a bigger change in air mass with highs\nwill be in the 70s with lows in the 50s to lower 60s for the latter \npart of the week.",
